From b22645d0dcfd24b516ed8b8f23b0dc4fd5c22038 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Hardin <kushastronaut@icloud.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 14:39:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Netplay speed: cheaper per-instruction checks, state digest
 and rollback loads

- A halted CPU runs no further instruction in a session slice (a native hook
  may stand in for a guest wait loop by entering HALT; the scheduler then
  parks the console until IE & IF, as for a guest HALTCNT store).
- multiplayer_slice_safe: the common cases (Thumb ROM code without memory
  access, ARM data processing and branches in IWRAM) are answered inline in
  the header; Thumb loads and stores are classified from the opcode bits
  instead of the decoded-instruction table. Same answers as before.
- State digests (every tick, about 0.8 MiB for two consoles): a streaming
  four-lane 64-bit hash over 32-byte blocks instead of byte-serial FNV-1a.
  Peers compare digests only within one build fingerprint.
- mp2k_detect keeps its result per ROM image: rollback loads build fresh
  buses, and without an MP2K driver the CRC fallback walked all of an 8 MiB
  ROM on each load.
- Guarded RAM-code templates read ordinary RAM straight from the active
  bus's backing store (published with the code view) and compare masked
  templates eight bytes at a time, prepared once per template.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
